1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
A group bought over five million lottery tickets and won twenty-seven million.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:11,180
This is part two. In nineteen ninety-two, an Australian fund tried to buy every ticket in Virginia's lottery.

3
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:14,800
And it worked. They hit the jackpot, a state record.

4
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:18,080
But the rule said tickets had to be bought at a licensed store.

5
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,900
And this group had millions of them printed in bulk.

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,480
So was the winning ticket legal? Hit like if you think Virginia should keep the money.

7
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:30,620
Virginia investigated for about three weeks, and nobody could prove how that ticket was bought.

8
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,160
No proof means no broken rule. So Virginia paid.

9
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:38,140
About one point three million dollars a year, for twenty years.

10
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:41,520
That's like twenty normal salaries, every single year.

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,460
Send this to the friend who says the house always wins.

12
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:48,820
Then Virginia rushed in new rules against bulk buying. Problem solved, right?

13
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:52,520
Thirty-one years later? Follow, 'cause this ending is wild.

14
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:57,280
In twenty twenty-three, a group bought nearly every combination in the Texas lottery.

15
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:00,000
And they won ninety-five million dollars.

16
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,780
It got so messy that Texas shut down its whole lottery commission.

17
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:08,360
So when someone buys every ticket, should the state have to pay? Yes or no?

